{
  "_comment": "Per-machine settings, created by install.py and never committed. plans.<vendor> (optional, written by hand; the installer writes none): none|free|pro|ultra (max reads as ultra; missing = pro for claude and openai, inferred from the available list for google: no fresh list = free; none removes the vendor, free moves it last in the default order, except the host vendor for design, implement and read), e.g. {\"google\": \"ultra\"}. A model whose min_plan in models.json (free|pro|ultra, default free; gemini-4-carbon is ultra) is above the vendor's plan is never routed, a role or task pin included. models.<id>.enabled: false hides a model, true skips the availability check. available: filled by models_collect.py --write-available (install runs it) with the ids each vendor CLI really offers; a vendor with no entry is assumed to offer everything. roles.<role> may set model, effort, vendor and grade, and hosts (a list of host names): with hosts the whole entry applies only on those hosts; without it, a design, implement or read pin of another vendor than the host's applies only on hosts whose first vendor it is. tasks.<name> = {\"about\": \"one line the orchestrator sees at session start\", \"base\": \"<a role: design|implement|review|read|translate>\", \"vendor\": ..., \"model\": ..., \"effort\": ..., \"grade\": ...} (only about and base are required) is a preference for one kind of work: route.py --task <name> routes as the base role, then prefers that vendor, model, effort or grade (effort missing = the grade table decides). A preference that cannot be used (exhausted, plan none, not available, CLI missing, the author's own vendor in a review) falls back to the base role and the result says task_applied false. codex.windows_sandbox: the value of codex -c windows.sandbox=<value> that run.py passes on Windows; missing = \"unelevated\", null = pass nothing [host: codex-cli 0.169.0 Windows, elevated -> CreateProcessWithLogonW 1385, 2026-11-17].",
  "models": {},
  "roles": {},
  "tasks": {}
}
